# R50 Three-Slot Effects Architecture

## Goal

Give R50 an effects architecture similar in spirit and scope to the Korg M1
without attempting a bit-exact M1 reimplementation.

R50 will have three selectable global effect slots, a small set of supported
routing topologies, and independent sends from each Partial. The effect
processors remain global: there is one instance of each selected slot for the
whole instrument, not one instance per voice or Partial.

The existing per-Partial waveshaper remains part of the synthesis voice and is
not moved into the global rack.

The Partial filter is deliberately digital and linear. It has cutoff,
resonance, slope, key tracking, and envelope modulation, but no Drive or
"Analogue" control. Nonlinear color belongs in the explicitly selectable
per-Partial waveshaper or global Overdrive/Distortion effects.

## Final Signal Model

Each voice renders Partial 1 and Partial 2 separately far enough to accumulate
their dry signal and effect sends. Sends from all active voices are then summed
into global stereo buses and processed by the three slots.

Conceptually:

```text
Partial 1 --+-- dry level ------------------------------+
            +-- Slot 1 send --+                         |
            +-- Slot 2 send --+--> global slot inputs   +--> output mix
            +-- Slot 3 send --+                         |
                                                          |
Partial 2 --+-- dry level ------------------------------+
            +-- Slot 1 send --+
            +-- Slot 2 send --+--> global slot inputs
            +-- Slot 3 send --+

output mix --> optional global compressor --> master gain
```

The rack supports these topologies:

1. Serial: `Slot 1 -> Slot 2 -> Slot 3`
2. Parallel: `Slot 1 + Slot 2 + Slot 3`
3. Serial pair plus parallel: `(Slot 1 -> Slot 2) + Slot 3`
4. Parallel pair into master: `(Slot 1 + Slot 2) -> Slot 3`

Do not implement a freely patchable graph in this project. Named topologies
make feedback ownership, latency, preset storage, UI, and testing predictable.

## Processing Ownership

### Per Partial and per voice

- Source/sample oscillator and noise
- Linear digital filter
- Amplitude, filter, and pitch envelopes
- Off, Soft Clip, Hard Clip, Fold, or Rectify waveshaper
- Waveshaper pre-filter/post-filter placement
- Level and pan
- Dry output level
- Slot 1, Slot 2, and Slot 3 send levels

### Global

- Slot 1 processor
- Slot 2 processor
- Slot 3 processor
- Slot routing topology
- Slot algorithm parameters and wet/output level
- Existing stereo-linked compressor, placed after the rack
- Master gain

Slot wet/output level and Partial sends have different jobs. A Partial send
controls how strongly that Partial excites a shared processor. Slot wet/output
level controls the processor return in the selected topology. Do not implement
Partial-specific wet/dry controls inside an algorithm.

## Effect Scope

Every slot can select any supported algorithm. Slots are not permanently
assigned to modulation, delay, or reverb.

### Initial adapters

Reuse the current implementations as the first three algorithms:

- Chorus
- Stereo/Cross Delay, including ping-pong behavior
- Reverb

### Target catalog

The completed rack should cover the following scope:

- Off
- Hall Reverb
- Room Reverb
- Plate/Stage Reverb with a selectable character
- Early Reflections
- Stereo Delay
- Cross/Ping-Pong Delay
- Chorus
- Symphonic Ensemble
- Flanger
- Phaser
- Tremolo/Auto-Pan
- Rotary Speaker
- Equalizer
- Overdrive
- Distortion
- Exciter

Variations such as multiple hall characters or phase-inverted modulation can
be algorithm modes rather than separate top-level algorithms. Combination
effects like the M1's Delay/Hall are composed with the three slots and do not
need dedicated algorithms.

The goal is comparable creative coverage and routing flexibility, not matching
the original M1's coefficients, nonlinear curves, modulation phases, or reverb
topologies.

## Researched Algorithm Definitions

This section is the implementation specification for the effect catalog. Names,
structures, modes, control meanings, and ranges below are decisions, not
placeholders. Implementations may be tuned by listening tests, but changing the
basic structure or public control contract requires updating this plan first.

The selected structures follow established audio-DSP practice:

- Schroeder established parallel feedback combs plus series allpasses as a
  practical reverberator structure; modern R50 reverbs will use the more
  controllable feedback-delay-network generalization. See
  [Natural Sounding Artificial Reverberation](https://aes2.org/publications/elibrary-page/?id=849).
- Dattorro documents recursive diffusion networks, delay modulation, damping,
  interpolation, and practical reverberator design in
  [Effect Design, Part 1](https://aes2.org/publications/elibrary-page/?id=10160).
- An FDN consists of recirculating delay lines and a mixing matrix. Dense input
  and output diffusion can improve echo buildup without a large line count, as
  shown in
  [Velvet-Noise Feedback Delay Network](https://www.dafx.de/paper-archive/details/5QRu1GCO7Aix-Qn4LMJdrw).
- Chorus and flanger are modulated-delay effects; a flanger forms moving,
  regularly spaced comb notches, while a phaser forms a limited number of
  moving notches using cascaded allpass sections. See
  [Enhanced Digital Models for Analog Modulation Effects](https://www.dafx.de/paper-archive/details.php?id=jPSmVsZq6Xn0nTNK6sygIQ)
  and
  [Modulation Extraction for LFO-Driven Audio Effects](https://dafx.de/paper-archive/2023/DAFx23_paper_63.pdf).
- Rotary-speaker perception needs synchronized Doppler delay, directional
  amplitude modulation, separate rotor speeds, and stereo projection. See the
  model in
  [Modulation and Delay Line Based Digital Audio Effects](https://dafx.de/paper-archive/1999/disch.pdf).
- EQ coefficient generation will use the bilinear-transform formulas in the
  W3C
  [Audio EQ Cookbook](https://www.w3.org/TR/audio-eq-cookbook/).
- Memoryless nonlinearities generate out-of-band harmonics which alias after
  sampling. R50 will combine oversampling with antiderivative antialiasing where
  useful, following
  [Reducing the Aliasing of Nonlinear Waveshaping Using Continuous-Time Convolution](https://www.dafx.de/paper-archive/details/vem_XXF5qBbfiWOH2RVVAA)
  and
  [Antialiasing Piecewise Polynomial Waveshapers](https://www.dafx.de/paper-archive/details/cu51KU_JsiXj1cvMi-2DOw).
- A harmonic exciter is modeled as a filtered high-frequency path, controlled
  distortion, and a blend with the direct signal. This is consistent with
  Shekar and Smith's
  [Modeling the Harmonic Exciter](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258333577_Modeling_the_Harmonic_Exciter).

### Common slot behavior

The following behavior is outside the individual algorithm controls and is
identical for all algorithms:

- `Algorithm`: exact selection from the roster below.
- `Bypass`: click-free dry pass-through for serial use and silence for a
  parallel return, as determined by the rack topology.
- `Input Gain`: -24 to +12 dB, default 0 dB.
- `Output Gain`: -24 to +12 dB, default 0 dB.
- `Mix`: 0 to 100 percent for insert/serial use. In a parallel topology the
  rack uses the algorithm's wet output and treats Mix as return level rather
  than adding a second dry path.
- `Stereo Width`: 0 to 200 percent where the algorithm produces stereo.

The rack must have one authoritative rule for dry-path ownership. An algorithm
must expose a separate wet result internally; it must not bake an unremovable
dry signal into its output.

### 0. Off

Structure:

- No algorithm state and no processing.
- Pass input unchanged when the slot is in a serial path.
- Produce silence when the slot is used as a parallel branch.

Controls:

- Common slot controls only.

### 1. Hall Reverb

Purpose:

- Smooth, spacious late reverberation with a slower density buildup and broad
  stereo image.

Structure:

- Stereo pre-delay.
- Twelve to sixteen sparse early-reflection taps derived from one deterministic
  seed.
- Input diffusion using two short modulated allpasses per channel.
- Eight-line FDN with mutually incommensurate delay lengths.
- Energy-preserving Hadamard or Householder feedback matrix.
- Per-line low-frequency decay control and one-pole high-frequency damping.
- Slow decorrelated delay modulation below audible vibrato depth.
- Different orthogonal projections for left and right returns.

Controls:

- `Pre-delay`: 0 to 200 ms, default 20 ms.
- `Decay`: 0.4 to 12 s RT60, default 3.2 s.
- `Size`: 50 to 200 percent, default 100 percent.
- `Damping`: 1 to 20 kHz, default 7 kHz.
- `Bass Decay`: 0.25 to 2 times the main decay, default 1.
- `Early Level`: 0 to 100 percent, default 35 percent.
- `Diffusion`: 0 to 100 percent, default 75 percent.
- `Modulation`: 0 to 100 percent, default 20 percent.

### 2. Room Reverb

Purpose:

- Short, perceptually bounded room ambience with prominent early reflections
  and a faster, less luxuriant tail than Hall.

Structure:

- Same shared early-reflection and FDN primitives as Hall.
- Sixteen to twenty-four earlier and more closely spaced reflection taps.
- Six- or eight-line FDN with shorter delay lengths.
- Higher early-to-late ratio and less delay modulation than Hall.
- Size changes reflection timing and FDN delays together.

Controls:

- `Pre-delay`: 0 to 80 ms, default 8 ms.
- `Decay`: 0.15 to 4 s RT60, default 1.1 s.
- `Size`: 25 to 150 percent, default 75 percent.
- `Damping`: 800 Hz to 20 kHz, default 6 kHz.
- `Bass Decay`: 0.25 to 2 times, default 0.8.
- `Early Level`: 0 to 100 percent, default 65 percent.
- `Diffusion`: 0 to 100 percent, default 55 percent.
- `Modulation`: 0 to 100 percent, default 8 percent.

### 3. Plate/Stage Reverb

Purpose:

- One algorithm with two discrete characters:
  `Plate` supplies a dense, bright, non-room-like tail; `Stage` supplies a wide
  performance-space response with clear early energy.

Structure:

- `Plate` mode: Dattorro-inspired figure-eight diffusion tank with input
  diffusion, two cross-coupled modulated tank loops, damping, and multiple
  stereo output taps.
- `Stage` mode: early-reflection generator feeding the eight-line FDN shared
  with Hall, using an asymmetric wide reflection pattern and less late
  diffusion.
- Both modes use the same public control layout and report zero host latency.

Controls:

- `Character`: Plate or Stage, default Plate.
- `Pre-delay`: 0 to 200 ms, default 12 ms.
- `Decay`: 0.3 to 12 s RT60, default 2.4 s.
- `Size`: 50 to 200 percent, default 100 percent.
- `Damping`: 1 to 20 kHz, default 9 kHz.
- `Bass Decay`: 0.25 to 2 times, default 0.9.
- `Early Level`: 0 to 100 percent; in Plate mode this controls input diffusion
  prominence, default 30 percent.
- `Diffusion`: 0 to 100 percent, default 85 percent.

### 4. Early Reflections

Purpose:

- Spatial definition, gated ambience, or reverse-style attacks without a late
  reverb wash.

Structure:

- Stereo multi-tap FIR delay with 24 taps.
- Deterministic families of nonuniform tap times, gains, polarity, damping, and
  pan.
- Four patterns: `Room`, `Hall`, `Gated`, and `Reverse`.
- No feedback loop and therefore a finite tail.
- Size scales tap times while preserving their relative pattern.

Controls:

- `Pattern`: Room, Hall, Gated, or Reverse, default Room.
- `Pre-delay`: 0 to 200 ms, default 5 ms.
- `Length`: 30 to 800 ms, default 180 ms.
- `Density`: 0 to 100 percent, default 65 percent.
- `Decay Shape`: -100 reverse/rising through 0 flat to +100 falling, with the
  selected pattern supplying the default.
- `Tone`: 500 Hz to 20 kHz low-pass, default 9 kHz.
- `Stereo Spread`: 0 to 200 percent, default 100 percent.

### 5. Stereo Delay

Purpose:

- Independent conventional left and right echoes with stable filtered
  feedback.

Structure:

- One fractional delay line per channel with four-point interpolation.
- Independent left/right delay times.
- Same-channel feedback.
- DC-blocking high-pass plus low-pass damping inside each feedback loop.
- Soft saturation inside feedback to bound transients without making unity
  feedback legal.
- Equal-power crossfade between read heads when delay time changes abruptly.

Controls:

- `Left Time`: 1 to 2000 ms or tempo division, default 250 ms.
- `Right Time`: 1 to 2000 ms or tempo division, default 375 ms.
- `Feedback`: -95 to +95 percent, default 35 percent; negative values invert
  feedback polarity.
- `Low Cut`: 20 Hz to 2 kHz, default 40 Hz.
- `High Cut`: 500 Hz to 20 kHz, default 8 kHz.
- `Saturation`: 0 to 100 percent, default 15 percent.
- `Time Mode`: milliseconds or tempo-synchronized, default milliseconds.

### 6. Cross/Ping-Pong Delay

Purpose:

- Echoes that regenerate between channels, continuously variable from straight
  stereo feedback to full ping-pong.

Structure:

- Same delay, interpolation, damping, saturation, and time-change behavior as
  Stereo Delay.
- A normalized 2x2 feedback matrix crossfades between diagonal
  same-channel feedback and off-diagonal cross-feedback without increasing the
  matrix gain.

Controls:

- `Left Time`: 1 to 2000 ms or tempo division, default 250 ms.
- `Right Time`: 1 to 2000 ms or tempo division, default 375 ms.
- `Feedback`: -95 to +95 percent, default 40 percent.
- `Cross`: 0 straight to 100 percent full ping-pong, default 100 percent.
- `Low Cut`: 20 Hz to 2 kHz, default 40 Hz.
- `High Cut`: 500 Hz to 20 kHz, default 7 kHz.
- `Saturation`: 0 to 100 percent, default 15 percent.
- `Time Mode`: milliseconds or tempo-synchronized, default milliseconds.

### 7. Chorus

Purpose:

- Natural pitch and timing thickening that can range from a restrained M1-style
  stereo chorus to a wide modern chorus.

Structure:

- Four fractional-delay voices, two projected to each output.
- Base delay longer than flanger: 8 to 30 ms.
- Sine or triangle primary LFO plus optional very-slow decorrelated drift.
- Quadrature and opposing phase offsets distribute voices across stereo.
- No regeneration at the public control level.

Controls:

- `Rate`: 0.03 to 10 Hz or tempo division, default 0.35 Hz.
- `Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, mapping to 0 to 5 ms excursion, default 40
  percent.
- `Delay`: 8 to 30 ms, default 17 ms.
- `Voices`: 2 or 4, default 4.
- `Waveform`: Sine or Triangle, default Sine.
- `Drift`: 0 to 100 percent, default 20 percent.
- `Tone`: 1 to 20 kHz wet-path low-pass, default 14 kHz.

### 8. Symphonic Ensemble

Purpose:

- Denser, less obviously periodic widening for strings, choir, brass, and pads.

Structure:

- Six fractional-delay voices, three per output.
- Three slow primary modulation rates in irrational ratios.
- Per-voice deterministic filtered random drift.
- Base delays from 12 to 28 ms.
- Cross-channel projection chosen for mono compatibility.
- Optional low-cut prevents low-frequency pitch smear.

Controls:

- `Rate`: 0.03 to 3 Hz, default 0.22 Hz.
- `Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 55 percent.
- `Spread`: 0 to 100 percent rate and phase dispersion, default 70 percent.
- `Drift`: 0 to 100 percent, default 45 percent.
- `Tone`: 1 to 20 kHz wet-path low-pass, default 12 kHz.
- `Low Cut`: 20 to 500 Hz, default 100 Hz.

### 9. Flanger

Purpose:

- Short modulated comb filtering from subtle animation to strong jet sweeps.

Structure:

- Fractional delay per channel with 0.1 to 15 ms delay.
- Dry plus delayed signal creates the feedforward comb.
- Bipolar feedback around each delay, with optional cross-feedback mode.
- Sine or triangle LFO; stereo phase offsets of 0, 90, or 180 degrees.
- Four-point interpolation and read-head smoothing.

Controls:

- `Rate`: 0.03 to 10 Hz or tempo division, default 0.2 Hz.
- `Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 65 percent.
- `Manual`: 0.1 to 15 ms center delay, default 2.5 ms.
- `Feedback`: -95 to +95 percent, default 35 percent.
- `Cross Feedback`: Off or On, default Off.
- `Waveform`: Sine or Triangle, default Sine.
- `Stereo Phase`: 0, 90, or 180 degrees, default 180 degrees.
- `Tone`: 1 to 20 kHz feedback low-pass, default 12 kHz.

### 10. Phaser

Purpose:

- A finite set of moving spectral notches with a character distinct from
  delay-line flanging.

Structure:

- Six or twelve first-order allpass stages per channel.
- LFO modulates allpass break frequencies exponentially so the sweep is
  perceptually even.
- Dry signal mixed with the allpass cascade produces the notches.
- Bipolar feedback from the cascade output to its input.
- Stereo channels use selectable LFO phase offset.
- Coefficients are smoothed and constrained strictly inside the stable region.

Controls:

- `Rate`: 0.03 to 10 Hz or tempo division, default 0.3 Hz.
- `Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 70 percent.
- `Center`: 80 Hz to 4 kHz, default 800 Hz.
- `Spread`: 0.5 to 6 octaves, default 3 octaves.
- `Feedback`: -95 to +95 percent, default 30 percent.
- `Stages`: 6 or 12, default 6.
- `Waveform`: Sine or Triangle, default Sine.
- `Stereo Phase`: 0 to 180 degrees, default 90 degrees.

### 11. Tremolo/Auto-Pan

Purpose:

- Periodic amplitude modulation, stereo movement, or a blend of both.

Structure:

- One band-limited LFO drives smoothed channel gains.
- `Tremolo` applies the same gain to both channels.
- `Auto-Pan` uses equal-power complementary gains.
- `Stereo Tremolo` permits controlled phase offset between channel gains.
- Shape morphs continuously from sine through triangle toward rounded square;
  do not use a discontinuous raw square wave.

Controls:

- `Mode`: Tremolo, Stereo Tremolo, or Auto-Pan, default Tremolo.
- `Rate`: 0.03 to 20 Hz or tempo division, default 4 Hz.
- `Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 60 percent.
- `Shape`: -100 soft sine through 0 triangle to +100 rounded square, default
  -100.
- `Stereo Phase`: 0 to 180 degrees, default 180 degrees in Stereo Tremolo.
- `Bias`: -100 to +100, changing the waveform duty emphasis without allowing
  gain below zero, default 0.

### 12. Rotary Speaker

Purpose:

- A musical Leslie-inspired effect rather than a measured model of a particular
  cabinet.

Structure:

- Linkwitz-Riley fourth-order crossover at an adjustable frequency.
- Independent horn and drum rotors.
- Each rotor uses two short time-varying delay paths for Doppler shift.
- Synchronized amplitude modulation represents directionality.
- Unequal left/right projection creates stereo rotation.
- Speed changes use separate acceleration and deceleration ramps; rotor phase
  remains continuous.
- A short cabinet/air damping stage follows the rotor mix.

Controls:

- `Speed`: Stop, Slow, or Fast, default Slow.
- `Slow Rate`: 0.2 to 1.5 Hz, default 0.7 Hz.
- `Fast Rate`: 3 to 10 Hz, default 6.5 Hz.
- `Horn/Drum Ratio`: 0.5 to 2, default 1.25.
- `Acceleration`: 0.2 to 8 s, default 1.8 s.
- `Doppler Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 55 percent.
- `Amplitude Depth`: 0 to 100 percent, default 65 percent.
- `Crossover`: 400 Hz to 2 kHz, default 800 Hz.

### 13. Equalizer

Purpose:

- Broad program-effect tone shaping comparable in role to the M1 EQ, with one
  useful mid band added.

Structure:

- Cascaded low-shelf, peaking, and high-shelf biquads.
- Coefficients use the Audio EQ Cookbook bilinear-transform formulas.
- Transposed direct-form II processing in double-precision state where useful.
- Parameter changes interpolate poles/zeros safely or crossfade coefficient
  sets; do not linearly sweep unstable raw coefficients.

Controls:

- `Low Gain`: -18 to +18 dB, default 0 dB.
- `Low Frequency`: 40 Hz to 1 kHz, default 200 Hz.
- `Mid Gain`: -18 to +18 dB, default 0 dB.
- `Mid Frequency`: 100 Hz to 10 kHz, default 1 kHz.
- `Mid Q`: 0.2 to 8, default 0.8.
- `High Gain`: -18 to +18 dB, default 0 dB.
- `High Frequency`: 1 to 16 kHz, default 5 kHz.

### 14. Overdrive

Purpose:

- Smooth, compressive predominantly odd-harmonic saturation with optional
  asymmetric even-harmonic content.

Structure:

- Input high-pass to prevent DC-driven bias shifts.
- Pre-emphasis low/high shelves.
- Four-times oversampled static soft-clipping stage.
- Transfer curve morphs between symmetric `tanh`-like clipping and an
  asymmetric diode-style polynomial/soft-clip curve.
- DC blocker after the nonlinearity.
- Post-emphasis tone filter and output compensation.
- Prefer first-order ADAA for the chosen analytic curve; retain oversampling
  because the complete pre/post-filtered structure is stateful.

Controls:

- `Drive`: 0 to 36 dB, default 12 dB.
- `Character`: Soft, Warm, or Bright, default Warm.
- `Asymmetry`: 0 to 100 percent, default 20 percent.
- `Tone`: 500 Hz to 16 kHz post low-pass mapping, default 7 kHz.
- `Bass`: -12 to +12 dB pre-emphasis, default 0 dB.
- `Level`: -24 to +12 dB, default -6 dB.

### 15. Distortion

Purpose:

- Harder, brighter clipping and fuzz-like sounds distinct from Overdrive.

Structure:

- Four-times oversampled nonlinear path, raised to eight-times only if
  aliasing acceptance tests require it.
- Three transfer modes: `Hard Clip`, `Cubic`, and `Fuzz`.
- Hard Clip and Cubic use piecewise-polynomial ADAA.
- Fuzz uses asymmetric clipping plus bias and must remove generated DC.
- Pre high-pass and post low-pass keep the result musically bounded.
- Output compensation is mode-specific.

Controls:

- `Drive`: 0 to 48 dB, default 18 dB.
- `Mode`: Hard Clip, Cubic, or Fuzz, default Cubic.
- `Bias`: -100 to +100, default 0.
- `Tone`: 500 Hz to 16 kHz post low-pass, default 6 kHz.
- `Low Cut`: 20 Hz to 1 kHz, default 60 Hz.
- `Level`: -30 to +12 dB, default -9 dB.

### 16. Exciter

Purpose:

- Add controlled high-frequency harmonics and presence without applying
  full-band distortion.

Structure:

- Split into a direct path and a second-order high-pass side path.
- Apply controllable drive and asymmetric soft clipping to the side path at
  four-times oversampling.
- Remove DC and band-limit the generated harmonics.
- Optionally delay the direct path by the oversampler/filter group delay so the
  blend remains phase-consistent.
- Add the processed side path back to the direct signal; the slot wet result is
  the complete aligned exciter output minus the aligned dry input.

Controls:

- `Frequency`: 1 to 10 kHz high-pass corner, default 3.5 kHz.
- `Drive`: 0 to 36 dB, default 12 dB.
- `Harmonics`: 0 to 100 percent asymmetry/curve amount, default 45 percent.
- `Amount`: 0 to 100 percent side-path blend, default 25 percent.
- `Tone`: 4 to 20 kHz side-path low-pass, default 14 kHz.
- `Presence`: -12 to +12 dB broad peaking stage, default 0 dB.

### Non-slot global compressor

The compressor remains a post-rack utility rather than a selectable slot
algorithm.

Structure:

- Stereo-linked feed-forward peak detector.
- 6 dB soft knee.
- Separate attack and release smoothing.
- No lookahead and therefore zero latency.
- Click-free bypass.

Public controls should be expanded from the current Amount macro to:

- `On`
- `Threshold`: -36 to 0 dBFS, default -18 dBFS.
- `Ratio`: 1:1 to 20:1, default 4:1.
- `Attack`: 1 to 100 ms, default 10 ms.
- `Release`: 20 to 1000 ms, default 120 ms.
- `Makeup`: 0 to 18 dB, default 0 dB.

R50 currently presents these settings through one post-rack `Amount` macro.

### Existing per-Partial waveshaper

The existing Off, Soft Clip, Hard Clip, Fold, and Rectify algorithms remain
per-Partial and retain pre-filter/post-filter placement. They are not aliases
for the global Overdrive or Distortion effects.

The Partial waveshaper now uses cascaded 4x oversampling built from 129-tap
Kaiser half-band FIR stages. It has approximately -86 dB rejection at the far
edge of the transition band, exact 96-sample base-rate round-trip latency, and
a latency-aligned lightweight bypass. Direct tests lock the 2x/4x impulse
latencies and DC gain; spectral acceptance tests remain mandatory for every
nonlinear algorithm. Before/after preset renders are still required because
the stronger reconstruction filter can subtly change the brightest patches.

## Shared Algorithm Acceptance Rules

All algorithms must satisfy these rules in addition to their individual
definition:

- Parameter values have identical audible meaning at every supported sample
  rate.
- Delay lengths are derived in seconds and resized during setup, never during
  rendering.
- Feedback matrices have a proven or bounded maximum gain below unity after
  user feedback is applied.
- Recursive state is checked or structurally prevented from producing NaN or
  infinity.
- Denormals are suppressed without injecting audible noise.
- Modulation phase remains continuous across rate and depth changes.
- Abrupt time changes use smoothing, interpolation, or dual-head crossfades.
- Tempo synchronization resolves to seconds at control rate and survives a
  missing or invalid host tempo.
- Stereo algorithms collapse acceptably to mono; width above 100 percent may
  reduce mono level but must not cancel the complete wet signal.
- Algorithm bypass and replacement preserve or intentionally fade the old
  tail; no state is abandoned as a full-scale discontinuity.
- Nonlinear algorithms pass swept-sine alias measurements before their
  oversampling factor is finalized.
- Every default is level-matched closely enough that selecting an algorithm
  does not cause a dangerous output jump.

## Slot DSP Contract

Create a common real-time-safe slot interface with:

- Setup for sample rate and maximum render size
- Reset
- Algorithm selection
- Parameter update at control rate
- Stereo processing
- Click-free bypass
- Click-free or short-crossfaded algorithm changes
- Tail-time reporting
- Latency reporting, even if all initial algorithms report zero
- No allocation, locking, file access, or ownership changes on the audio thread

Prefer shared primitives for:

- Fractional delay lines
- LFO and waveform generation
- Wet/dry and return gain smoothing
- Low/high effect EQ
- Feedback damping
- Parameter smoothing

Each slot owns only the state for its active algorithm unless profiling shows
that pre-instantiating all algorithms is cheaper and simpler. Algorithm changes
must prepare required storage outside the audio callback or use storage sized
during setup.

## Parameter Model

Parameter addresses are append-only. Do not renumber or reinterpret existing
addresses because presets and host automation depend on them.

Add global parameters for:

- Slot 1 algorithm and bypass
- Slot 2 algorithm and bypass
- Slot 3 algorithm and bypass
- Rack topology
- Slot output/wet level
- A stable bank of algorithm controls per slot

Add Partial fields for:

- Dry level
- Slot 1 send
- Slot 2 send
- Slot 3 send

Use stable generic storage for slot controls, but present algorithm-specific
names, units, ranges, and value formatting in the UI. Document the mapping for
every algorithm in one authoritative descriptor table rather than duplicating
switch statements across DSP, Audio Unit parameters, and SwiftUI.

Suggested normalized control capacity per slot:

- Eight continuous controls
- Two or more discrete/mode controls

If an algorithm needs more controls, extend the append-only bank rather than
overloading an unrelated parameter.

## Factory preset architecture

Factory presets are authored directly against:

```text
Slot 1 Chorus -> Slot 2 Delay -> Slot 3 Reverb -> Compressor
```

Preset defaults:

- Topology: Serial
- Partials route at unity to Slot 1 and have no duplicate direct path.
- Chorus, delay, and reverb use Slots 1, 2, and 3 respectively when combined.
- Compressor remains post-rack.

R50 has not shipped, so prototype compatibility code and retired parameter
addresses are intentionally absent. Saved state contains only the three-slot
model and post-rack compressor.

## UI

Replace the fixed Chorus, Delay, Reverb, and Compressor columns with:

- A topology selector and compact routing diagram
- Three slot panels
- Algorithm selector and bypass per slot
- Dynamic controls appropriate to the selected algorithm
- Slot return/output level
- A separate post-rack compressor panel

On each Partial page add:

- Dry level
- Send 1
- Send 2
- Send 3

Use consistent slot colors or labels in both the Partial sends and effects
page. Make the current routing visible without requiring the user to infer it
from control values.

Changing algorithms should update labels and ranges without destroying the
stored parameters for other algorithms during the current editing session.

## Implementation Phases

### Phase 1 - Data model and routing skeleton

- [x] Define algorithm and topology enums.
- [x] Define slot descriptors and the real-time DSP contract.
- [x] Append global slot parameters and Partial send fields.
- [x] Extend parameter snapshots and Audio Unit parameter exposure.
- [x] Make each voice expose or accumulate separate Partial contributions.
- [x] Add global dry and three-slot input buses.
- [x] Implement the four named routing topologies with passthrough slots.
- [x] Confirm that the unchanged default produces the current dry render.

Exit criteria:

- Both Partials can be independently routed to dry and each slot input.
- All four topologies pass deterministic impulse-routing tests.
- The render callback remains allocation-free.

### Phase 2 - Adapt existing effects

- [x] Adapt Chorus to the slot interface.
- [x] Adapt Delay to the slot interface.
- [x] Adapt Reverb to the slot interface.
- [x] Move the compressor after the three-slot rack.
- [x] Add smoothed bypass, sends, returns, and topology changes.
- [x] Preserve effect tails when notes stop.

Exit criteria:

- Any of the three existing algorithms can run in any slot.
- Serial, parallel, and mixed topologies behave correctly.
- Switching or bypassing a slot produces no objectionable click.

### Phase 3 - State model and initial UI

- [x] Store and restore the three-slot state directly.
- [x] Author factory presets directly with slot parameters.
- [x] Build the three-slot effects page.
- [x] Add topology visualization.
- [x] Add Partial dry and send controls.
- [x] Keep compressor controls separate from the slots.

Exit criteria:

- Old presets load successfully.
- New presets round-trip every slot, topology, send, and algorithm parameter.
- Factory preset comparison renders remain acceptably close.

### Phase 4 - Modulation family

- [x] Add Ensemble.
- [x] Add Flanger.
- [x] Add Phaser.
- [x] Add Tremolo/Auto-Pan.
- [x] Add Rotary Speaker.
- [x] Share LFO, fractional delay, smoothing, and effect-EQ primitives.

Exit criteria:

- Every modulation algorithm is stable across supported sample rates.
- Stereo width and mono compatibility have automated checks.
- Bipolar feedback and waveform modes are tested.

### Phase 5 - Space and delay family

- [x] Add Hall, Room, and Plate/Stage characters.
- [x] Add Early Reflections.
- [x] Separate Stereo Delay and Cross/Ping-Pong modes cleanly.
- [x] Add pre-delay, damping, effect EQ, and independent channel timing where
      appropriate.

Exit criteria:

- Delay feedback remains bounded at every allowed value.
- Reverb and early-reflection tails decay to silence without denormals.
- Tail-time reporting is conservative enough for the host.

### Phase 6 - Tone and nonlinear family

- [x] Add Equalizer.
- [x] Add Overdrive.
- [x] Add Distortion.
- [x] Add Exciter.
- [x] Apply oversampling where aliasing tests show it is necessary.
- [x] Distinguish these global algorithms clearly from the existing
      per-Partial waveshaper.

Measured optimized cost for one stereo 4x Distortion slot at 192 kHz is
approximately 0.07 seconds per second of audio on the development machine.
Overdrive, Distortion, and Exciter reuse the 129-tap 4x oversampler whose
latency, DC gain, and alias-rejection tests also cover the per-Partial shaper.

Exit criteria:

- Nonlinear algorithms remain finite for full-scale and pathological input.
- Bypass and unity settings do not create unexpected level jumps.
- Aliasing and CPU costs are documented and tested.

### Phase 7 - Optimization and polish

- [x] Benchmark maximum polyphony at all supported sample rates and buffer
      sizes.
- [x] Remove the old fixed rack from the runtime signal path and host parameter
      tree. Its small DSP primitives remain only as isolated test fixtures.
- [x] Tune smoothing and transition times, including exact first-sample bypass
      at zero Mix and click-free algorithm/topology transitions.
- [x] Add hover help for routing, sends, and every algorithm.
- [x] Review factory presets and add examples for modulation, rotary, EQ,
      overdrive, and exciter alongside the existing space/delay examples.
- [x] Audit dynamic controls against DSP use. Remove the unsupported reverb,
      EQ, overdrive, distortion, and exciter controls; wire Plate/Stage and
      Overdrive Character so every remaining visible control changes DSP.
- [x] Remove obsolete state conversion and retired host parameters.

Validation covers the full eight-voice engine, all algorithms from 11.025 to
192 kHz, zero-mix identity, effect audibility/tails, pathological finite input,
concurrent automation under ThreadSanitizer, and 4x nonlinear processing at
192 kHz. The measured worst-case standalone stereo Distortion cost is about
0.10x realtime on the development machine.

Exit criteria:

- No real-time allocations, locks, NaNs, infinities, or denormal regressions.
- Maximum-polyphony CPU stays within the agreed product budget.
- The catalog, UI, saved state, and documentation agree.

## Test Plan

Add focused DSP tests for:

- Impulse routing through all four topologies
- Partial 1 and Partial 2 send isolation
- Dry-level and send gain accuracy
- Slot order and parallel return summing
- Slot bypass at silence and during sustained audio
- Algorithm changes during sustained audio
- Tail continuation after note-off
- Reset clearing all delay and filter state
- Independent engine instances producing independent effect state
- Deterministic rendering where algorithms are deterministic
- Bipolar and cross-feedback polarity
- Feedback stability at maximum settings
- Mono input/output compatibility
- Sample rates from the lowest supported rate through 192 kHz
- Small and large host buffer sizes
- Silence, impulse, DC, full-scale, and non-finite-defense inputs
- New-state and factory-preset round trips
- Maximum-polyphony CPU and memory use

## Primary Files Expected to Change

- `Products/R50/DSP/R50EffectsRack.hpp`
- `Products/R50/DSP/R50Engine.hpp`
- `Products/R50/DSP/R50Voice.hpp`
- `Products/R50/DSP/R50Partial.hpp`
- `Products/R50/Extension/R50Parameters.h`
- `Products/R50/Extension/R50ParametersTree.swift`
- `Products/R50/Extension/R50DSPKernelAdapter.mm`
- `Products/R50/Extension/UI/R50ParameterModel.swift`
- `Products/R50/Extension/UI/R50View.swift`
- `Products/R50/Extension/R50FactoryPresets.swift`
- `Tests/test_r50.cpp`

New DSP headers should separate shared primitives, algorithm implementations,
slot hosting, and rack routing when `R50EffectsRack.hpp` would otherwise become
an unreviewable monolith.

## Definition of Done

The work is complete when:

- R50 exposes three interchangeable global effect slots.
- All four named routing topologies work.
- Each Partial has independent dry and three-slot sends.
- The target effect families are present and usable in any slot.
- The compressor remains an optional post-rack global processor.
- The per-Partial waveshaper remains independent of the rack.
- New presets and automation restore all effects state exactly.
- DSP safety, routing, tail, and CPU tests pass.
- The UI clearly communicates slots, topology, sends, and global versus
  per-Partial processing.
